<%# The roster: what the reader follows, what they have muted, and the way to
    add another.

    Drawn on the Subscriptions page rather than on a page of its own, because
    this is the same question the sections above it answer — what reaches me,
    and what does not. A second page would be a second answer.

    Both kinds of source in one list, each row drawn through its own template,
    the way the editions archive holds an edition and a gap. The form adds
    blogs only: a sender is not followed, they turn up, so the only decision
    there is to record about one is the mute that put them here.

    One shape as the pen's sections, so the type scale hangs off the section
    name in CSS and no template asks which one it is drawing. %>
<section class="pen__section pen__section--blogs" id="blogs">
  <h2 class="pen__heading"><%= t(".heading") %></h2>

  <%= form_with model: blog, url: blogs_path, class: "sources__form" do |form| %>
    <%= form.label :feed_url, t(".label"), class: "sources__label" %>

    <%# Focused only when a follow was refused — when the reader submitted an
        address themselves and has one thing to do next, here.

        Never when the field was filled from the query, however much the
        ergonomics want it. A filled field one keystroke from following is a
        link any page can hand a signed-in reader: "press Enter to continue",
        and the follow goes through as an ordinary first-party POST carrying
        their own cookie and token. What that follows fetches from inside the
        network and puts a stranger's writing in front of the editor every
        morning after. The section carries an id so a bookmarklet gets the
        scroll it wanted from the anchor instead.

        Not on an ordinary visit either: the reader came to read the pen, and
        moving focus unasked is worse than useless on a screen reader. %>
    <div class="sources__controls">
      <%= form.url_field :feed_url,
            required: true,
            autocomplete: "off",
            autofocus: blog.errors.any?,
            placeholder: t(".placeholder"),
            class: "sources__input" %>
      <%= form.submit t(".submit"), class: "sources__submit" %>
    </div>

    <%# The reason, in the sentence the model wrote it in. A refused feed is
        the one moment there is to say why, so it says why here rather than
        "is invalid". %>
    <% blog.errors.full_messages_for(:feed_url).each do |message| %>
      <p class="sources__error"><%= message %></p>
    <% end %>

    <p class="sources__note"><%= t(".note") %></p>
  <% end %>

  <% if sources.any? %>
    <ol class="pen__list"><%= render sources %></ol>
  <% else %>
    <p class="pen__empty"><%= t(".empty") %></p>
  <% end %>
</section>
